WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:10.126
This video explains the key differentiation rules that you will need from now on. With them, we find the derivative directly from the function term.

2
00:00:11.626 --> 00:00:17.926
We obtain the derivative as a new function and can then calculate the slope of the tangent at any point.

3
00:00:19.726 --> 00:00:38.823
We go through the rules in this order: first some basic building blocks – constant functions, powers, sums and constant factors, the exponential and the logarithmic function. These are followed by three rules for combined functions: for products, for quotients and for composite functions.

4
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:47.651
We start with the simplest function: a constant function, for example f of x equals three.

5
00:00:48.651 --> 00:00:56.099
Its graph is a horizontal line. However x changes, the function value stays the same. The slope is zero everywhere.

6
00:00:56.899 --> 00:01:02.348
This holds for every fixed number b: the derivative of a constant function is zero.

7
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.249
Next, we look at power functions.

8
00:01:08.849 --> 00:01:17.182
Here, the function term is a power. The base is x, and the exponent n can be any whole number or any decimal number, except zero.

9
00:01:18.382 --> 00:01:26.389
The power rule says: for the derivative, the exponent is written in front as a factor, and we subtract one from the exponent.

10
00:01:27.589 --> 00:01:38.122
Let us look at the function f of x equals x cubed as an example. In the derivative, the three comes to the front as a factor, and we reduce the exponent by one.

11
00:01:38.822 --> 00:01:42.824
As the derivative, we therefore get three x squared.

12
00:01:44.024 --> 00:01:52.492
Next example: the function f of x equals x. Here, too, we can use the power rule if we write x as x to the power of one.

13
00:01:53.492 --> 00:01:58.128
The power rule reduces the exponent by one, that is, to zero.

14
00:01:58.928 --> 00:02:05.087
Since any number to the power of zero equals one, the result of the derivative is one.

15
00:02:06.087 --> 00:02:10.776
Accordingly, the line y equals x has slope one everywhere.

16
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:18.766
The power rule also works in situations where, at first glance, it does not seem to apply.

17
00:02:19.366 --> 00:02:23.581
What about, for example, the function one over x?

18
00:02:24.581 --> 00:02:32.957
A first rewriting rule helps here: a fraction with x to the power of n in the denominator becomes x to the power of minus n.

19
00:02:34.157 --> 00:02:39.798
With the rewriting rule, one over x becomes x to the power of minus one.

20
00:02:40.798 --> 00:02:47.434
Now we apply the power rule: the minus one comes to the front as a factor, and we subtract one from the exponent.

21
00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:53.519
In the exponent, we get minus one minus one, that is, minus two.

22
00:02:54.319 --> 00:03:00.707
Written back as a fraction with the first rule, the derivative is therefore minus one over x squared.

23
00:03:01.907 --> 00:03:07.756
Next example: How can we differentiate the function f of x equals the square root of x?

24
00:03:08.756 --> 00:03:20.689
In this situation, a second rewriting rule helps: a root becomes a fraction in the exponent. The exponent under the root goes to the top of the fraction, the small number on the root to the bottom.

25
00:03:21.889 --> 00:03:26.257
With the second rule, the square root of x equals x to the power of one half.

26
00:03:27.257 --> 00:03:33.983
Now we apply the power rule: one half comes to the front as a factor, and we subtract one from the exponent.

27
00:03:34.783 --> 00:03:39.876
In the exponent, we get one half minus one, that is, minus one half.

28
00:03:40.676 --> 00:03:46.774
Written back as a root, this gives one over two times the square root of x.

29
00:03:47.974 --> 00:03:51.976
In the third example, we use both rewriting rules one after the other.

30
00:03:52.776 --> 00:04:01.599
First, we rewrite the term under the root. The first rule is helpful here. Instead of dividing by x squared, we write x to the power of minus two.

31
00:04:02.399 --> 00:04:07.858
With the second rule, its cube root is x to the power of minus two thirds.

32
00:04:09.358 --> 00:04:13.528
With the new notation, we can now find the derivative using the power rule.

33
00:04:14.228 --> 00:04:19.582
Minus two thirds comes to the front as a factor, and we subtract one from the exponent.

34
00:04:20.382 --> 00:04:30.055
In the exponent, we get minus two thirds minus one. That is minus two thirds minus three thirds, so minus five thirds.

35
00:04:30.855 --> 00:04:37.463
So overall, the derivative is minus two thirds times x to the power of minus five thirds.

36
00:04:38.463 --> 00:04:45.668
Written back as a root, this gives minus two over three times the cube root of x to the power of five.

37
00:04:46.868 --> 00:04:58.783
Rewriting the derivative as a fraction and a root is optional and not required in the exam. For finding higher derivatives, the power notation from the second-to-last step would be more convenient.

38
00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:02.723
Now we come to sums.

39
00:05:03.723 --> 00:05:10.208
If a function consists of several terms, we differentiate each term separately and add the results.

40
00:05:11.208 --> 00:05:15.707
As an example, we consider the function x cubed plus x squared.

41
00:05:16.507 --> 00:05:22.294
The first term is differentiated with the power rule. This gives three x squared.

42
00:05:23.095 --> 00:05:27.217
Differentiated in the same way, the second term gives two x.

43
00:05:28.017 --> 00:05:32.415
So the derivative of f is the sum of these two results.

44
00:05:34.415 --> 00:05:41.880
A special case is a term that does not depend on x at all: an additive constant, for example in x squared plus three.

45
00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:46.112
By the power rule, x squared becomes two x.

46
00:05:46.812 --> 00:05:52.253
The constant three has the derivative zero. So it vanishes when we differentiate.

47
00:05:53.453 --> 00:06:05.002
The graph shows why: the constant only shifts the curve upwards. As a result, the curve becomes neither steeper nor flatter at any point. The tangent is simply shifted along and keeps its slope.

48
00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.836
Now we look at a constant factor in front of a function.

49
00:06:14.836 --> 00:06:20.201
If a function is multiplied by a constant factor, this factor is kept when differentiating.

50
00:06:21.201 --> 00:06:24.898
For three times x squared, the three stays.

51
00:06:25.698 --> 00:06:30.138
x squared is differentiated with the power rule and gives two x.

52
00:06:30.938 --> 00:06:33.823
Together, this gives the derivative six x.

53
00:06:35.823 --> 00:06:45.955
On the graph: the factor three makes every function value three times as large. So every change in the function value is also three times as large, and so is the slope.

54
00:06:46.755 --> 00:06:50.561
That is why the factor is also kept in the derivative.

55
00:06:51.761 --> 00:06:56.051
Here we see two memory aids for the additive and the multiplicative constant.

56
00:06:57.251 --> 00:07:03.798
Both types of constant can appear in one function, for example in four x cubed plus five.

57
00:07:04.598 --> 00:07:11.507
The factor four stays when differentiating, and x cubed becomes three x squared by the power rule.

58
00:07:12.307 --> 00:07:18.483
The additive constant five vanishes. Overall, the derivative is twelve x squared.

59
00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:27.688
Two functions have particularly memorable derivatives: the exponential function e to the x and the natural logarithm.

60
00:07:28.688 --> 00:07:32.228
The derivative of e to the x is again e to the x.

61
00:07:33.028 --> 00:07:37.680
So at every point, the slope is exactly as large as the function value.

62
00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.277
At x equals zero, both are one.

63
00:07:43.077 --> 00:07:47.512
At x equals one, both are about two point seven two.

64
00:07:48.512 --> 00:08:01.548
This leads to exponential growth: the function value grows and is at the same time the slope. So the slope keeps growing too, and this in turn makes the function values grow faster and faster.

65
00:08:03.548 --> 00:08:07.874
The natural logarithm is only defined for positive x.

66
00:08:08.674 --> 00:08:12.726
The derivative of the natural logarithm is one over x.

67
00:08:13.726 --> 00:08:17.162
At x equals one, the slope is one.

68
00:08:17.962 --> 00:08:23.755
At x equals two, it is only one half. So the curve becomes flatter and flatter.

69
00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:29.642
Now we come to functions that are made up of two parts.

70
00:08:30.642 --> 00:08:34.360
First, we look at a function made of two factors.

71
00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:39.439
Here is an example: f of x equals x times ln x.

72
00:08:40.239 --> 00:08:44.984
With the differentiation rules discussed so far, we cannot find the derivative.

73
00:08:45.784 --> 00:08:51.782
The factors that appear in the function both depend on x, and we write them down as helper functions.

74
00:08:52.382 --> 00:09:00.898
The first factor is u of x equals x, the second factor is v of x equals ln x.

75
00:09:01.898 --> 00:09:10.286
For the derivative of a product, the product rule applies. It builds the derivative from the helper functions and their derivatives as follows.

76
00:09:10.786 --> 00:09:17.151
u' of x times v of x, plus u of x times v' of x.

77
00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:25.883
For our example, we already know the derivatives: u' of x is one, and v' of x is one over x.

78
00:09:26.883 --> 00:09:31.317
Substituting gives first one times ln x.

79
00:09:31.917 --> 00:09:35.749
To this we add x times one over x.

80
00:09:36.549 --> 00:09:43.534
In the second term, the x cancels. So the derivative is ln x plus one.

81
00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:48.877
Next, we look at quotients of two functions.

82
00:09:49.877 --> 00:09:56.249
Here u is in the numerator and v in the denominator, and the denominator must not be zero.

83
00:09:57.049 --> 00:10:03.549
In the derivative, the numerator contains almost the same expression as in the product rule, but with a minus in the middle.

84
00:10:04.349 --> 00:10:15.466
That is why the order matters here: first comes u' times v, then u times v' is subtracted. The denominator is v squared.

85
00:10:16.666 --> 00:10:25.130
As an example, we take e to the x over x, for x not equal to zero. Here u equals e to the x and v equals x.

86
00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:31.111
The derivative of u is again e to the x, the derivative of v is one.

87
00:10:32.111 --> 00:10:40.261
Substituting, we get in the numerator e to the x times x minus e to the x times one, and in the denominator x squared.

88
00:10:41.061 --> 00:10:45.510
In the numerator, we factor out e to the x and obtain the result.

89
00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:50.431
Finally, we look at composite functions.

90
00:10:51.431 --> 00:10:55.453
Here we substitute the result of one function into another function.

91
00:10:56.253 --> 00:11:00.550
An example is the logarithm of x squared plus three x.

92
00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:08.318
When we evaluate this function for a specific value of x, we proceed, as we know, in two steps.

93
00:11:08.918 --> 00:11:11.656
Let us take x equals one, for example.

94
00:11:12.256 --> 00:11:22.829
To calculate f of x, we first carry out the calculation inside the brackets of the logarithm, for x equals one that is one plus three equals four.

95
00:11:23.429 --> 00:11:26.268
Then we apply the logarithm to this value.

96
00:11:27.268 --> 00:11:31.354
Following these two steps, we split the function into two parts.

97
00:11:32.154 --> 00:11:37.161
We call the first calculation step the inner function, v of x for short.

98
00:11:37.761 --> 00:11:42.512
The inner function often also sits visually inside the function term.

99
00:11:43.512 --> 00:11:47.898
We obtain the outer function by writing just v in place of the inner function.

100
00:11:48.498 --> 00:11:54.158
So the outer function is a function of v. Here u of v equals ln v.

101
00:11:55.158 --> 00:11:59.044
For the chain rule, we need the derivatives of both helper functions.

102
00:11:59.644 --> 00:12:03.651
In the example, the derivative of the logarithm is one over v.

103
00:12:04.451 --> 00:12:09.292
For the inner derivative, we use the power rule and get two x plus three.

104
00:12:10.292 --> 00:12:18.763
According to the chain rule, we obtain the whole derivative f'(x) as the product of the outer derivative and the inner derivative.

105
00:12:19.963 --> 00:12:26.497
In the example, this product is one over v times, in brackets, two x plus three.

106
00:12:27.497 --> 00:12:35.570
In the last step, for v we have to put the right-hand side of the inner function back in, so that the overall result depends only on x.

107
00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:40.316
Written as a fraction, two x plus three is in the numerator.

108
00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:49.838
The most important differentiation rules can be found in the formula sheet. You can practise them in the quiz that follows.

109
00:12:50.838 --> 00:12:57.944
When we later deal with functions of several variables, the two rules from the memory aids in particular will play a role again.
